TRANSPORT/CELLULAR HYPOTHESIS RULED OUT 2026-08-12 (Dan asked: 'possible I downloaded these before allowing rediff on cellular, but it was on wifi all day'). Answered from source + the empty table, so nobody re-tests it:

A REFUSED TRANSPORT GATE LEAVES A ROW. DayZeroRediffTrigger.swift:244-249:
    guard transportDecision.isAllowed else {
        if let exit = transportDecision.deniedExit { await suppressionRecorder(analysisAssetId, exit, now) }
        return SweepSummary()
    }
and suppressionRecorder -> PlayheadRuntime.swift:1697 -> AnalysisStore.noteRediffDayZeroSuppression (:7855) -> INSERT INTO rediff_day_zero_attempts. The comment at :234-243 says this was built for precisely this question: 'A REFUSED GATE IS NO LONGER SILENT... on a device where day-0 never ran, the gate, a dropped wiring and a flag that was off were indistinguishable from each other. Each refusal now names itself in rediff_day_zero_attempts.lastExit.' (playhead-4dqe)

rediff_day_zero_attempts IS EMPTY. So the gate never refused — it was never asked.

AND THE ORDERING SEALS IT: kg8h's claim is written at RediffDayZeroKickoffCoordinator.swift:170; triggerIfEligible runs later, inside process(). A transport refusal would have left TWO kinds of evidence — a claim row AND an attempt row. Neither exists. The failure is UPSTREAM OF THE TRIGGER ENTIRELY.

So all five deny legs are out: denyDisabled, denyUnreachable, denyLowDataMode, denyCellularNotAllowed, denyPower.

WORTH KNOWING ANYWAY, surfaced by the question: denyPower is 'unplugged with no deep-scan opt-in', and it denies REGARDLESS of network. An unplugged phone on wifi can still decline day-0. Not the cause here, but it is a real leg and it is not obvious from the user-facing framing of the setting as a wifi-vs-cellular choice.

H1/H2 UNCHANGED: the claim write threw and was swallowed by try?, or requestKickoff never ran. Still indistinguishable. The one-line fix in this bead's description is still what separates them.

description:
FOUND in playhead-rouw R2 review, 2026-08-13, attacking the canary R1 built. VERIFIED by probe, not reasoned: two files, `xcodebuild test-without-building -only-testing:PlayheadTests/BackgroundSessionEnumerationSourceCanaryTests`, rc=0.

`BackgroundSessionEnumerationSourceCanaryTests` counts four spellings of "ask a URLSession what tasks it holds" across every `.swift` under `Playhead/`, permitting exactly one occurrence inside `DownloadManager.boundedAllTasks`. Three of the four (`.allTasks`, `getAllTasks`, `getTasksWithCompletionHandler`) are counted unconditionally. The fourth, `.tasks`, is narrowed — it is only counted in a file that mentions `URLSession` — because `.tasks` is a plausible member name on types with nothing to do with networking.

THE ESCAPE. A file that reaches the session through a `typealias` declared in ANOTHER file never spells `URLSession`, so the narrowing never arms:

    // Playhead/Services/Downloads/RouwProbeAlias.swift   — passes: no `.tasks`
    typealias RouwBackgroundSession = URLSession

    // Playhead/Services/Downloads/RouwProbeInventory.swift — passes: no `URLSession`
    enum RouwBackgroundTransferInventory {
        static func liveDescriptions(of session: RouwBackgroundSession) async -> [String] {
            let (_, _, downloads) = await session.tasks
            return downloads.compactMap { $0.taskDescription }
        }
    }

rc=0. The whole never-resuming await, back, in exactly the shape of the three probes that defeated the canary's FIRST version (`withCheckedContinuation { session.getAllTasks {...} }`, `await session.tasks`, and a file not on the audit list).

WHY IT IS P3 AND NOT P2. It takes a deliberate act — a typealias for `URLSession` that does not exist in the tree today — and the three unconditional tokens are the idiomatic spellings. It is a limit, not an open door. Recorded here so the next reviewer does not re-derive it, and so nobody reads the rail's "one identifier, every spelling" claim as covering `.tasks`.

WHAT R2 DID FIX, and it is adjacent. The narrowing read the RAW source, so any file that merely NAMED `URLSession` in a comment had `.tasks` armed against it — a probe holding `batch.tasks.count` on a plain struct, with the framework type in a "see also" line and no networking at all, FAILED the rail claiming it "asks a URLSession for its task list 1 time(s)" (rc=65). The predicate deciding WHETHER to count was reading a different population from the count itself. It now reads the STRIPPED code; that probe is rc=0, and a true-positive probe (`await session.tasks` with `URLSession` named in code) is still rc=65.

THE TWO WAYS TO CLOSE THIS, and both cost something, which is why it is filed rather than guessed:
  * Count `.tasks` unconditionally. Closes the escape; brings the false positive above back for any future `scheduler.tasks` / `batch.tasks`. Measured cost today is zero — there are ZERO `.tasks` occurrences under `Playhead/` — so this is cheap NOW and gets more expensive as the tree grows.
  * Resolve types. Correct, and far beyond what a `String`-walking canary can do; it would mean a real parser (SwiftSyntax) for one token.

A third option worth considering instead of either: require any file that names a background `URLSession` to obtain it through a single named accessor, the way playhead-x0lb's `RegionFillDoor` turned "the spellings the last reviewer thought of" into a compile-enforced naming obligation. That is a production change, not a test one.
